  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 206 AM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cyclonic northwest flow will prevail under an upper- \nlevel low this weekend. A potent embedded shortwave will pass south \ntoday and tonight, bringing widespread showers. While they will be \nmost concentrated in the typical northwest flow upslope areas, very \nmarginal instability will develop in the lowest 10,000 ft in the \natmosphere due to cold air aloft and solar heating. This will cause \nthe precipitation to trend more convective, especially this \nafternoon. Therefore, snow showers will be able to survive crossing \nthe terrain and should pass through the downslope areas like the \nConnecticut River Valley, though likely in a lighter form. While \ntemperatures will rise above freezing below 2,000 ft and likely \nreach 40 in the lowest points, temperatures will drop significantly \nin any of the showers. Therefore, even though consistent snow levels \nwill likely only be down to around 1,000-1,500 ft, a period of \ngraupel or even brief wet snow will be possible all the way down \ninto the valleys. However, any continuous accumulations should \ngenerally be limited to above 2,000 ft. The showers will gradually \nbecome less and less widespread heading into Sunday, and they should \nbe done by Sunday evening. Accumulations by the end should be in the \n1-4 inch range above 2,000 ft, with mostly brief coatings that melt \nbelow that. The lowest accumulating snow levels will be tonight. \nMoisture off Lake Champlain will enhance the showers in parts of the \nChamplain Valley and Greens, but the more convective nature may \nprevent the formation of distinct bands at times.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A potent ridge and associated area of surface high \npressure will dominate the weather next week, making it feel as if \nit were still mid-October. In a classic El Nino pattern, deep \ntroughing and strong surface lows over the Pacific have caused \ncontinuous southwest to westerly flow out west, flooding the Great \nPlains and western Canada with warm Pacific air. This has caused \ntemperature anomalies in the 20-30+ degree range. This airmass will \nbegin to arrive Monday, but clouds will likely remain stuck under \nthe inversion, so it will likely take until Tuesday to warm up. From \nthen through the end of the week, and potentially through the \nweekend, highs in the 50s will prevail, though 60 will be possible \nin some of the warmest areas. There will be a strong, persistent \nsubsidence inversion, and it will aided by the fact that \nsignificantly more surface heat will be lost at night than will be \nable to be replenished by the sun during the day. Radiational \ncooling will help lows drop to the 20s and 30s, around \nclimatological normals. Weak troughs will gradually erode the ridge \nfrom the north, but it will take until at least next weekend for it \nto get pushed to the south and east and for cooler conditions and \nchances for precipitation to return.",
